---
title: PowerSync Collection
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync collections provide seamless integration between TanStack DB and "},{"type":"link","href":"https://powersync.com","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync"}]},{"type":"text","value":", enabling automatic synchronization between your in-memory TanStack DB collections and PowerSync's SQLite database. This gives you offline-ready persistence, real-time sync capabilities, and powerful conflict resolution."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection"},{"type":"text","value":" package allows you to create collections that:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatically mirror the state of an underlying PowerSync SQLite database"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactively update when PowerSync records change"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Support query-driven sync, loading only the data relevant to active live queries"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Support optimistic mutations with rollback on error"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide persistence handlers to keep PowerSync in sync with TanStack DB transactions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use PowerSync's efficient SQLite-based storage engine"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Work with PowerSync's real-time sync features for offline-first scenarios"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leverage PowerSync's built-in conflict resolution and data consistency guarantees"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enable real-time synchronization with PostgreSQL, MongoDB and MySQL backends"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"1-installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Installation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install the PowerSync collection package along with your preferred framework integration.\nPowerSync currently works with Web, React Native and Node.js. The examples below use the Web SDK.\nSee the PowerSync quickstart "},{"type":"link","href":"https://docs.powersync.com/installation/quickstart-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"docs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for more details."}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/powersync-db-collection @powersync/web @journeyapps/wa-sqlite","lang":"bash"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"2-create-a-powersync-database-and-schema","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Create a PowerSync Database and Schema"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Schema, Table, column } from \"@powersync/web\"\n\n// Define your schema\nconst APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  documents: new Table({\n    name: column.text,\n    author: column.text,\n    created_at: column.text,\n    archived: column.integer,\n  }),\n})\n\n// Initialize PowerSync database\nconst db = new PowerSyncDatabase({\n  database: {\n    dbFilename: \"app.sqlite\",\n  },\n  schema: APP_SCHEMA,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"3-optional-configure-sync-with-a-backend","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. (optional) Configure Sync with a Backend"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type {\n  CommonPowerSyncDatabase,\n  PowerSyncBackendConnector,\n  PowerSyncCredentials,\n} from \"@powersync/web\"\n\n// TODO implement your logic here\nclass Connector implements PowerSyncBackendConnector {\n  fetchCredentials: () => Promise<PowerSyncCredentials | null>\n\n  /** Upload local changes to the app backend.\n   *\n   * Use {@link CommonPowerSyncDatabase.getCrudBatch} to get a batch of changes to upload.\n   *\n   * Any thrown errors will result in a retry after the configured wait period (default: 5 seconds).\n   */\n  uploadData: (database: CommonPowerSyncDatabase) => Promise<void>\n}\n\n// Configure the client to connect to a PowerSync service and your backend\ndb.connect(new Connector())","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"4-create-a-tanstack-db-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Create a TanStack DB Collection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two main ways to create a collection: using type inference or using schema validation. Type inference will infer collection types from the underlying PowerSync SQLite tables. Schema validation can be used for additional input/output validations and type transforms."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"option-1-using-table-type-inference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option 1: Using Table Type Inference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The collection types are automatically inferred from the PowerSync schema table definition. The table is used to construct a default standard schema validator which is used internally to validate collection operations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection mutations accept SQLite types and queries report data with SQLite types."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: string | null // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number | null // SQLite integer\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The standard PowerSync SQLite types map to these TypeScript types:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync Column Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Description"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.text"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string | null"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text values, commonly used for strings, JSON, dates (as ISO strings)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.integer"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number | null"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Integer values, also used for booleans (0/1)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.real"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number | null"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Floating point numbers"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: All PowerSync column types are nullable by default."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"option-2-sqlite-types-with-schema-validation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option 2: SQLite Types with Schema Validation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Additional validations for collection mutations can be performed with a custom schema. The Schema below asserts that\nthe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"author"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"created_at"},{"type":"text","value":" fields are required as input. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" also has an additional string length check."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: The input and output types specified in this example still satisfy the underlying SQLite types. An additional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deserializationSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" is required if the typing differs. See the examples below for more details."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schema"},{"type":"text","value":". Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDeserializationError"},{"type":"text","value":" handler must be provided to react to this case."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\nimport { z } from \"zod\"\n\n// Schema validates SQLite types but adds constraints\nconst schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string().min(3, { message: \"Should be at least 3 characters\" }),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.string(), // SQLite TEXT for dates\n  archived: z.number(),\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string\n  author: string\n  created_at: string // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number // SQLite integer\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"option-3-transform-sqlite-input-types-to-rich-output-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option 3: Transform SQLite Input Types to Rich Output Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can transform SQLite types to richer types (like Date objects) while keeping SQLite-compatible input types:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: The Transformed types are provided by TanStack DB to the PowerSync SQLite persister. These types need to be serialized in\norder to be persisted to SQLite. Most types are converted by default. For custom types, override the serialization by providing a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serializer"},{"type":"text","value":" param."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The example below uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nullable"},{"type":"text","value":" columns, this is not a requirement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schema"},{"type":"text","value":". Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDeserializationError"},{"type":"text","value":" handler must be provided to react to this case."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string().nullable(),\n  created_at: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? new Date(val) : null)), // Transform SQLite TEXT to Date\n  archived: z\n    .number()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val != null ? val > 0 : null)), // Transform SQLite INTEGER to boolean\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n    // Optional: custom column serialization\n    serializer: {\n      // Dates are serialized by default, this is just an example\n      created_at: (value) => (value ? value.toISOString() : null),\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: string | null // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number | null\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: Date | null // JS Date instance\n  archived: boolean | null // JS boolean\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"option-4-custom-input-output-types-with-deserialization","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option 4: Custom Input/Output Types with Deserialization"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The input and output types can be completely decoupled from the internal SQLite types. This can be used to accept rich values for input mutations.\nWe require an additional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deserializationSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" in order to validate and transform incoming synced (SQLite) updates. This schema should convert the incoming SQLite update to the output type."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deserializationSchema"},{"type":"text","value":". Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDeserializationError"},{"type":"text","value":" handler must be provided to react to this case."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Our input/output types use Date and boolean\nconst schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string(),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.date(), // Accept Date objects as input\n  archived: z.boolean(), // Accept Booleans as input\n})\n\n// Schema to transform from SQLite types to our output types\nconst deserializationSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string(),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z\n    .string()\n    .transform((val) => (new Date(val))), // SQLite TEXT to Date\n  archived: z\n    .number()\n    .transform((val) => (val > 0), // SQLite INTEGER to Boolean\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    deserializationSchema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string\n  author: string\n  created_at: Date\n  archived: boolean\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"features","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"offline-first","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Offline-First"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync collections are offline-first by default. All data is stored locally in a SQLite database, allowing your app to work without an internet connection. Changes are automatically synced when connectivity is restored."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"real-time-sync","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Real-Time Sync"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When connected to a PowerSync backend, changes are automatically synchronized in real-time across all connected clients. The sync process handles:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bi-directional sync with the server"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conflict resolution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queue management for offline changes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic retries on connection loss"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"query-driven-sync","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query-Driven Sync"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collections support an optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand"},{"type":"text","value":" sync mode that loads only the rows your active live queries need, rather than syncing the full table into memory upfront. Useful for large datasets where most rows won't be accessed at a given time."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"working-with-rich-javascript-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Working with Rich JavaScript Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync collections support rich JavaScript types like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Boolean"},{"type":"text","value":", and custom objects while maintaining SQLite compatibility. The collection handles serialization and deserialization automatically:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { z } from \"zod\"\nimport { Schema, Table, column } from \"@powersync/web\"\nimport { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\n// Define PowerSync SQLite schema\nconst APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  tasks: new Table({\n    title: column.text,\n    due_date: column.text, // Stored as ISO string in SQLite\n    completed: column.integer, // Stored as 0/1 in SQLite\n    metadata: column.text, // Stored as JSON string in SQLite\n  }),\n})\n\n// Define rich types schema\nconst taskSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  title: z.string().nullable(),\n  due_date: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? new Date(val) : null)), // Convert to Date\n  completed: z\n    .number()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val != null ? val > 0 : null)), // Convert to boolean\n  metadata: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? JSON.parse(val) : null)), // Parse JSON\n})\n\n// Create collection with rich types\nconst tasksCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.tasks,\n    schema: taskSchema,\n  })\n)\n\n// Work with rich types in your code\nawait tasksCollection.insert({\n  id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n  title: \"Review PR\",\n  due_date: \"2025-10-30T10:00:00Z\", // String input is automatically converted to Date\n  completed: 0, // Number input is automatically converted to boolean\n  metadata: JSON.stringify({ priority: \"high\" }),\n})\n\n// Query returns rich types\nconst task = tasksCollection.get(\"task-1\")\nconsole.log(task.due_date instanceof Date) // true\nconsole.log(typeof task.completed) // \"boolean\"\nconsole.log(task.metadata.priority) // \"high\"","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"type-safety-with-rich-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Safety with Rich Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The collection maintains type safety throughout:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type TaskInput = {\n  id: string\n  title: string | null\n  due_date: string | null // Accept ISO string for mutations\n  completed: number | null // Accept 0/1 for mutations\n  metadata: string | null // Accept JSON string for mutations\n}\n\ntype TaskOutput = {\n  id: string\n  title: string | null\n  due_date: Date | null // Get Date object in queries\n  completed: boolean | null // Get boolean in queries\n  metadata: {\n    priority: string\n    [key: string]: any\n  } | null\n}\n\n// TypeScript enforces correct types:\ntasksCollection.insert({\n  due_date: new Date(), // Error: Type 'Date' is not assignable to type 'string'\n})\n\nconst task = tasksCollection.get(\"task-1\")\ntask.due_date.getTime() // OK - TypeScript knows this is a Date","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"optimistic-updates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optimistic Updates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Updates to the collection are applied optimistically to the local state first, then synchronized with PowerSync and the backend. If an error occurs during sync, the changes are automatically rolled back."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"metadata-tracking","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata Tracking"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata tracking allows attaching custom metadata to collection operations (insert, update, delete). This metadata is persisted alongside the operation and available in PowerSync "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CrudEntry"},{"type":"text","value":" records during upload processing. This is useful for passing additional context about mutations to the backend, such as audit information, operation sources, or custom processing hints."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"enabling-metadata-tracking","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enabling Metadata Tracking"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata tracking must be enabled on the PowerSync table:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  documents: new Table(\n    {\n      name: column.text,\n      author: column.text,\n    },\n    {\n      // Enable metadata tracking on this table\n      trackMetadata: true,\n    }\n  ),\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"using-metadata-in-operations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Metadata in Operations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once enabled, metadata can be passed to any collection operation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const documents = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n  })\n)\n\n// Insert with metadata\nawait documents.insert(\n  {\n    id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n    name: \"Report Q4\",\n    author: \"Jane Smith\",\n  },\n  {\n    metadata: {\n      source: \"web-app\",\n      userId: \"user-123\",\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\n    },\n  }\n).isPersisted.promise\n\n// Update with metadata\nawait documents.update(\n  docId,\n  { metadata: { reason: \"typo-fix\", editor: \"user-456\" } },\n  (doc) => {\n    doc.name = \"Report Q4 (Updated)\"\n  }\n).isPersisted.promise\n\n// Delete with metadata\nawait documents.delete(docId, {\n  metadata: { deletedBy: \"user-789\", reason: \"duplicate\" },\n}).isPersisted.promise","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"accessing-metadata-during-upload","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessing Metadata During Upload"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The metadata is available in PowerSync "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CrudEntry"},{"type":"text","value":" records when processing uploads in the connector:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { CommonPowerSyncDatabase } from \"@powersync/web\"\n\nclass Connector implements PowerSyncBackendConnector {\n  // ...\n\n  async uploadData(database: CommonPowerSyncDatabase) {\n    const batch = await database.getCrudBatch()\n    if (!batch) return\n\n    for (const entry of batch.crud) {\n      console.log(\"Operation:\", entry.op) // PUT, PATCH, DELETE\n      console.log(\"Table:\", entry.table)\n      console.log(\"Data:\", entry.opData)\n      console.log(\"Metadata:\", entry.metadata) // Custom metadata (stringified)\n\n      // Parse metadata if needed\n      if (entry.metadata) {\n        const meta = JSON.parse(entry.metadata)\n        console.log(\"Source:\", meta.source)\n        console.log(\"User ID:\", meta.userId)\n      }\n\n      // Process the operation with the backend...\n    }\n\n    await batch.complete()\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note"}]},{"type":"text","value":": If metadata is provided to an operation but the table doesn't have "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trackMetadata: true"},{"type":"text","value":", a warning will be logged and the metadata will be ignored."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"powerSyncCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" function accepts the following options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface PowerSyncCollectionConfig<TTable extends Table, TSchema> {\n  // Required options\n  database: PowerSyncDatabase\n  table: Table\n\n  // Schema validation and type transformation\n  schema?: StandardSchemaV1\n  deserializationSchema?: StandardSchemaV1 // Required for custom input types\n  onDeserializationError?: (error: StandardSchemaV1.FailureResult) => void // Required for custom input types\n\n  // Optional Custom serialization\n  serializer?: {\n    [Key in keyof TOutput]?: (value: TOutput[Key]) => SQLiteCompatibleType\n  }\n\n  // Performance tuning\n  syncBatchSize?: number // Control batch size for initial sync, defaults to 1000\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"advanced-transactions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced Transactions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you need more control over transaction handling, such as batching multiple operations or handling complex transaction scenarios, you can use PowerSync's transaction system directly with TanStack DB transactions."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { PowerSyncTransactor } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\n// Create a transaction that won't auto-commit\nconst batchTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Use PowerSyncTransactor to apply the transaction to PowerSync\n    await new PowerSyncTransactor({ database: db }).applyTransaction(\n      transaction\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// Perform multiple operations in the transaction\nbatchTx.mutate(() => {\n  // Add multiple documents in a single transaction\n  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    documentsCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      name: `Document ${i}`,\n      content: `Content ${i}`,\n      created_at: new Date().toISOString(),\n      updated_at: new Date().toISOString(),\n    })\n  }\n})\n\n// Commit the transaction\nawait batchTx.commit()\n\n// Wait for the changes to be persisted\nawait batchTx.isPersisted.promise","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This approach allows you to:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Batch multiple operations into a single transaction"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Control when the transaction is committed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ensure all operations are atomic"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wait for persistence confirmation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handle complex transaction scenarios"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"on-demand-sync-mode-query-driven-sync","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand Sync Mode (Query-Driven Sync)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default the PowerSync collection uses the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eager"},{"type":"text","value":" sync mode, but you can opt to use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand"},{"type":"text","value":" sync mode instead. In on-demand mode, the collection is query-driven - it only loads data from SQLite that satisfies the predicates of your active live queries, rather than syncing everything and filtering afterwards."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To understand how data flows under the hood, it helps to know that there are two independent dimensions at play:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sync mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eager"},{"type":"text","value":" vs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand"},{"type":"text","value":") - controls how data moves from SQLite into the TanStack DB collection"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sync Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (optional) - controls how data moves from the PowerSync Service into SQLite, using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoad"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" hooks"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These dimensions compose independently, giving you six combinations covered below:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sync mode"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sync Streams"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parameters"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eager"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"-"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"-"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eager"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hardcoded"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hardcoded"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extractSimpleComparisons"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parseWhereExpression"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples 1 and 2 assume SQLite is already fully populated by PowerSync's sync mechanism. Examples 3–6 add Sync Streams, which let you control which data from the PowerSync Service reaches SQLite in the first place - useful when you only want a subset of data on-device."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In examples 3 and 4, Sync Stream subscription parameters are hardcoded. Examples 5 and 6 show how to derive those parameters dynamically from the live query's predicates, so the subscription automatically adjusts as the query changes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"1-eager-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Eager Mode"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eager"},{"type":"text","value":" mode, all data from the SQLite database is synced into the TanStack DB collection. The live query then filters this full dataset to produce the final result. This means the collection holds all rows regardless of whether they match any active query."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eager mode syncs all SQLite data to TanStack DB Collection"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'eager'\n  }),\n)\n\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"2-on-demand-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. On-Demand Mode"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand"},{"type":"text","value":" mode, only data from the SQLite database that satisfies the predicates of active TanStack DB live queries is synced into the TanStack DB collection. This reduces the amount of data held in memory, since the collection only contains rows that are relevant to the queries currently registered against it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list_id = 'list_1'"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand'\n  }),\n)\n\n// live query that tells the collection what the data domain is\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"incorporating-sync-streams-with-onload-onloadsubset-hooks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Incorporating Sync Streams With OnLoad/OnLoadSubset Hooks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sync Streams can be used through the data loading hooks for both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eager"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand"},{"type":"text","value":" sync modes that allow a user to call Sync Streams when a collection is defined (eager mode) or when a collection's data boundary changes based on the live queries predicates (on-demand)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These examples assume the following Sync Stream definition exists:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"config:\n  edition: 3\n\nstreams:\n  lists:\n    query: SELECT * FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id()\n    auto_subscribe: true\n  todos:\n    query: SELECT * FROM todos WHERE list_id = subscription.parameter('list') AND list_id IN (SELECT id FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id())"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"3-eager-mode-with-sync-streams","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. Eager Mode with Sync Streams"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoad"},{"type":"text","value":" hook to subscribe to a Sync Stream when the collection first loads, so SQLite is populated before the collection starts serving queries. In "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eager"},{"type":"text","value":" mode, all rows in SQLite are synced into the TanStack DB collection; live query filtering then runs against that full dataset.\nThe hook can optionally return a cleanup function where you can unsubscribe from the Sync Stream."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, only 2 of which get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Because it's eager mode, both get synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query only returns the single todo that matches the live query predicate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PS Service"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list_id = 'list_1'"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eager mode syncs all SQLite data to TanStack DB Collection"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB Query filters for completed = 1"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'eager',\n    onLoad: async () => {\n      console.log('onLoad')\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: 'list_1' })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log('onUnload')\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n) \n\n// A live query that filters by the `completed` state.\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.completed, 1))\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"4-on-demand-mode-with-sync-streams","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. On-Demand Mode with Sync Streams"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" hook to subscribe to a Sync Stream whenever the collection's data boundary changes (i.e. when the set of active live queries changes). In "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand"},{"type":"text","value":" mode, only rows that satisfy the active live query predicates are synced from SQLite into the TanStack DB collection.\nThe hook can optionally return a cleanup function where you can unsubscribe from the Sync Stream when that subset is no longer needed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, only 2 of which get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Because it's on-demand mode, only 1 todo matches gets synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query only returns the single todo that matches the live query predicate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PS Service"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list_id = 'list_1'"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: completed = 1"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB Query filters for completed = 1"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      console.log('onLoadSubset')\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: 'list_1' })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log('onUnloadSubset')\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// A live query that filters by the `completed` state.\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.completed, 1))\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"5-on-demand-mode-with-simple-predicates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5. On-Demand Mode with Simple Predicates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the previous example, the Sync Stream subscription parameters were hardcoded. In practice, you'll often want to derive those parameters dynamically from the live query's where clause. That way, changing the query automatically adjusts which data is synced from the PowerSync Service. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extractSimpleComparisons"},{"type":"text","value":" is a convenience helper that parses the expression tree from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" into a flat list of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ field, operator, value }"},{"type":"text","value":" objects."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Given a live query like:"}]},{"type":"code","value":".where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, selectedListId))"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" receives options.where as an expression tree "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"for eq(list_id, '<uuid>')"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list_id"},{"type":"text","value":" value is parsed from the expression tree and passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncStream()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: This example slightly differs from the previous two as it aims to illustrate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extractSimpleComparisons"},{"type":"text","value":" usage."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, the Sync Stream subscription criteria ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list_id = \"list_1\""},{"type":"text","value":") is derived from the live query registered against the collection. Only 2 todos get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Two todos get synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query returns both todos as they both match "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id')"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PS Service"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Derive the list_id criteria from live query, subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list = 'list_1'"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list_id = 'list_1'"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      // Extract simple comparisons from the where expression\n      const comparisons = extractSimpleComparisons(options.where)\n      // comparisons = [{ field: ['todo', 'list_id'], operator: 'eq', value: '<uuid>' }]\n\n      // Find the list_id filter\n      const listIdFilter = comparisons.find(\n        (c) => c.field.includes('list_id') && c.operator === 'eq',\n      )\n\n      if (!listIdFilter) {\n        console.warn('No list_id filter found, skipping Sync Stream')\n        return\n      }\n\n      console.log(`Subscribing to todos for list: ${listIdFilter.value}`)\n\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: listIdFilter.value })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log(`Unsubscribing from todos for list: ${listIdFilter.value}`)\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// Simple filter -> triggers `onLoadSubset` with `eq(list_id, '...')`\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'), // or some listId variable\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"6-on-demand-mode-with-complex-predicates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6. On-Demand Mode with Complex Predicates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extractSimpleComparisons"},{"type":"text","value":" works well for simple, single-condition filters, live queries often combine multiple conditions using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"and"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"or"},{"type":"text","value":", or other nested expressions. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parseWhereExpression"},{"type":"text","value":" lets you provide custom handler functions for each operator ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"and"},{"type":"text","value":", etc.). This gives you full control over how a compound expression tree is unpacked and transformed into the parameters object passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncStream()"},{"type":"text","value":". It's useful when your Sync Stream accepts multiple parameters and you need to extract all of them from a single composite where clause - for example, filtering by both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list_id"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":" status simultaneously."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assume a small adjustment to the Sync Stream definition of todos (adding the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":" subscription parameter):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"todos:\n    query: SELECT * FROM todos WHERE list_id = subscription.parameter('list') AND completed = subscription.parameter(\"completed\") AND list_id IN (SELECT id FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id())"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter name is kept as-is (consistent with most examples), but must be mapped to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list_id"},{"type":"text","value":" to work with the following example. Alternatively, it can be named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list_id"},{"type":"text","value":" in the Sync Stream definition to skip the programmatic mapping step."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, the Sync Stream subscription criteria ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list_id = \"list_1\" and completed = 1"},{"type":"text","value":") is derived from the live query registered against the collection. Only 1 todo gets synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. One todo gets synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query returns 1 todo that matches "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id') and eq(todo.completed, 1)"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PS Service"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Derive the list_id and completed criteria from live query, subscribe to Sync Stream where list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB Query filters for list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}]},{"type":"table","align":["center","center","center"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list_1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      // Parse the where into a flat params record using custom handlers\n      const streamParams = parseWhereExpression(options.where, {\n        handlers: {\n          eq: (field: Array<string>, value: unknown) => {\n            const mappedField = mapFields(field[field.length - 1]!)\n\n            return {\n              [mappedField]: value,\n            }\n          },\n          and: (...filters: Array<Record<string, unknown>>) =>\n            Object.assign({}, ...filters),\n        },\n        onUnknownOperator: (op, _args) => {\n          console.warn(`Ignoring unsupported operator in stream params: ${op}`)\n          return {}\n        },\n      })\n      // For a query like: where(({ todo }) => and(eq(todo.list_id, 'abc'), eq(todo.completed, 0)))\n      // streamParams = { list: 'abc', completed: 0 }\n\n      if (!streamParams || Object.keys(streamParams).length === 0) {\n        console.warn('No stream params extracted, skipping Sync Stream')\n        return\n      }\n\n      console.log(\n        `Subscribing to todos with params: ${JSON.stringify(streamParams)}`,\n      )\n\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', streamParams)\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => subscription.unsubscribe()\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// Compound filter -> triggers `onLoadSubset` with `and(eq(list_id, '...'), eq(completed, 1))`\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) =>\n        and(eq(todo.list_id, 'list_1'), eq(todo.completed, 1)),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})","lang":"typescript"}],"headings":[{"id":"overview","text":"Overview","level":2},{"id":"1-installation","text":"1. Installation","level":2},{"id":"2-create-a-powersync-database-and-schema","text":"2. Create a PowerSync Database and Schema","level":3},{"id":"3-optional-configure-sync-with-a-backend","text":"3. (optional) Configure Sync with a Backend","level":3},{"id":"4-create-a-tanstack-db-collection","text":"4. Create a TanStack DB Collection","level":3},{"id":"option-1-using-table-type-inference","text":"Option 1: Using Table Type Inference","level":4},{"id":"option-2-sqlite-types-with-schema-validation","text":"Option 2: SQLite Types with Schema Validation","level":4},{"id":"option-3-transform-sqlite-input-types-to-rich-output-types","text":"Option 3: Transform SQLite Input Types to Rich Output Types","level":4},{"id":"option-4-custom-input-output-types-with-deserialization","text":"Option 4: Custom Input/Output Types with Deserialization","level":4},{"id":"features","text":"Features","level":2},{"id":"offline-first","text":"Offline-First","level":3},{"id":"real-time-sync","text":"Real-Time Sync","level":3},{"id":"query-driven-sync","text":"Query-Driven Sync","level":3},{"id":"working-with-rich-javascript-types","text":"Working with Rich JavaScript Types","level":3},{"id":"type-safety-with-rich-types","text":"Type Safety with Rich Types","level":3},{"id":"optimistic-updates","text":"Optimistic Updates","level":3},{"id":"metadata-tracking","text":"Metadata Tracking","level":3},{"id":"enabling-metadata-tracking","text":"Enabling Metadata Tracking","level":4},{"id":"using-metadata-in-operations","text":"Using Metadata in Operations","level":4},{"id":"accessing-metadata-during-upload","text":"Accessing Metadata During Upload","level":4},{"id":"configuration-options","text":"Configuration Options","level":2},{"id":"advanced-transactions","text":"Advanced Transactions","level":2},{"id":"on-demand-sync-mode-query-driven-sync","text":"On-demand Sync Mode (Query-Driven Sync)","level":2},{"id":"1-eager-mode","text":"1. Eager Mode","level":3},{"id":"2-on-demand-mode","text":"2. On-Demand Mode","level":3},{"id":"incorporating-sync-streams-with-onload-onloadsubset-hooks","text":"Incorporating Sync Streams With OnLoad/OnLoadSubset Hooks","level":2},{"id":"3-eager-mode-with-sync-streams","text":"3. Eager Mode with Sync Streams","level":3},{"id":"4-on-demand-mode-with-sync-streams","text":"4. On-Demand Mode with Sync Streams","level":3},{"id":"5-on-demand-mode-with-simple-predicates","text":"5. On-Demand Mode with Simple Predicates","level":3},{"id":"6-on-demand-mode-with-complex-predicates","text":"6. On-Demand Mode with Complex Predicates","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nPowerSync collections provide seamless integration between TanStack DB and [PowerSync](https://powersync.com), enabling automatic synchronization between your in-memory TanStack DB collections and PowerSync's SQLite database. This gives you offline-ready persistence, real-time sync capabilities, and powerful conflict resolution.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `@tanstack/powersync-db-collection` package allows you to create collections that:\n\n- Automatically mirror the state of an underlying PowerSync SQLite database\n- Reactively update when PowerSync records change\n- Support query-driven sync, loading only the data relevant to active live queries\n- Support optimistic mutations with rollback on error\n- Provide persistence handlers to keep PowerSync in sync with TanStack DB transactions\n- Use PowerSync's efficient SQLite-based storage engine\n- Work with PowerSync's real-time sync features for offline-first scenarios\n- Leverage PowerSync's built-in conflict resolution and data consistency guarantees\n- Enable real-time synchronization with PostgreSQL, MongoDB and MySQL backends\n\n## 1. Installation\n\nInstall the PowerSync collection package along with your preferred framework integration.\nPowerSync currently works with Web, React Native and Node.js. The examples below use the Web SDK.\nSee the PowerSync quickstart [docs](https://docs.powersync.com/installation/quickstart-guide) for more details.\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/powersync-db-collection @powersync/web @journeyapps/wa-sqlite\n```\n\n### 2. Create a PowerSync Database and Schema\n\n```ts\nimport { Schema, Table, column } from \"@powersync/web\"\n\n// Define your schema\nconst APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  documents: new Table({\n    name: column.text,\n    author: column.text,\n    created_at: column.text,\n    archived: column.integer,\n  }),\n})\n\n// Initialize PowerSync database\nconst db = new PowerSyncDatabase({\n  database: {\n    dbFilename: \"app.sqlite\",\n  },\n  schema: APP_SCHEMA,\n})\n```\n\n### 3. (optional) Configure Sync with a Backend\n\n```ts\nimport type {\n  CommonPowerSyncDatabase,\n  PowerSyncBackendConnector,\n  PowerSyncCredentials,\n} from \"@powersync/web\"\n\n// TODO implement your logic here\nclass Connector implements PowerSyncBackendConnector {\n  fetchCredentials: () => Promise<PowerSyncCredentials | null>\n\n  /** Upload local changes to the app backend.\n   *\n   * Use {@link CommonPowerSyncDatabase.getCrudBatch} to get a batch of changes to upload.\n   *\n   * Any thrown errors will result in a retry after the configured wait period (default: 5 seconds).\n   */\n  uploadData: (database: CommonPowerSyncDatabase) => Promise<void>\n}\n\n// Configure the client to connect to a PowerSync service and your backend\ndb.connect(new Connector())\n```\n\n### 4. Create a TanStack DB Collection\n\nThere are two main ways to create a collection: using type inference or using schema validation. Type inference will infer collection types from the underlying PowerSync SQLite tables. Schema validation can be used for additional input/output validations and type transforms.\n\n#### Option 1: Using Table Type Inference\n\nThe collection types are automatically inferred from the PowerSync schema table definition. The table is used to construct a default standard schema validator which is used internally to validate collection operations.\n\nCollection mutations accept SQLite types and queries report data with SQLite types.\n\n```ts\nimport { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: string | null // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number | null // SQLite integer\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput\n```\n\nThe standard PowerSync SQLite types map to these TypeScript types:\n\n| PowerSync Column Type | TypeScript Type  | Description                                                          |\n| --------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `column.text`         | `string \\| null` | Text values, commonly used for strings, JSON, dates (as ISO strings) |\n| `column.integer`      | `number \\| null` | Integer values, also used for booleans (0/1)                         |\n| `column.real`         | `number \\| null` | Floating point numbers                                               |\n\nNote: All PowerSync column types are nullable by default.\n\n#### Option 2: SQLite Types with Schema Validation\n\nAdditional validations for collection mutations can be performed with a custom schema. The Schema below asserts that\nthe `name`, `author` and `created_at` fields are required as input. `name` also has an additional string length check.\n\nNote: The input and output types specified in this example still satisfy the underlying SQLite types. An additional `deserializationSchema` is required if the typing differs. See the examples below for more details.\n\nThe application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the `schema`. Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An `onDeserializationError` handler must be provided to react to this case.\n\n```ts\nimport { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\nimport { z } from \"zod\"\n\n// Schema validates SQLite types but adds constraints\nconst schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string().min(3, { message: \"Should be at least 3 characters\" }),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.string(), // SQLite TEXT for dates\n  archived: z.number(),\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string\n  author: string\n  created_at: string // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number // SQLite integer\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput\n```\n\n#### Option 3: Transform SQLite Input Types to Rich Output Types\n\nYou can transform SQLite types to richer types (like Date objects) while keeping SQLite-compatible input types:\n\nNote: The Transformed types are provided by TanStack DB to the PowerSync SQLite persister. These types need to be serialized in\norder to be persisted to SQLite. Most types are converted by default. For custom types, override the serialization by providing a\n`serializer` param.\n\nThe example below uses `nullable` columns, this is not a requirement.\n\nThe application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the `schema`. Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An `onDeserializationError` handler must be provided to react to this case.\n\n```ts\nconst schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string().nullable(),\n  created_at: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? new Date(val) : null)), // Transform SQLite TEXT to Date\n  archived: z\n    .number()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val != null ? val > 0 : null)), // Transform SQLite INTEGER to boolean\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n    // Optional: custom column serialization\n    serializer: {\n      // Dates are serialized by default, this is just an example\n      created_at: (value) => (value ? value.toISOString() : null),\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: string | null // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number | null\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: Date | null // JS Date instance\n  archived: boolean | null // JS boolean\n}\n```\n\n#### Option 4: Custom Input/Output Types with Deserialization\n\nThe input and output types can be completely decoupled from the internal SQLite types. This can be used to accept rich values for input mutations.\nWe require an additional `deserializationSchema` in order to validate and transform incoming synced (SQLite) updates. This schema should convert the incoming SQLite update to the output type.\n\nThe application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the `deserializationSchema`. Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An `onDeserializationError` handler must be provided to react to this case.\n\n```ts\n// Our input/output types use Date and boolean\nconst schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string(),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.date(), // Accept Date objects as input\n  archived: z.boolean(), // Accept Booleans as input\n})\n\n// Schema to transform from SQLite types to our output types\nconst deserializationSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string(),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z\n    .string()\n    .transform((val) => (new Date(val))), // SQLite TEXT to Date\n  archived: z\n    .number()\n    .transform((val) => (val > 0), // SQLite INTEGER to Boolean\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    deserializationSchema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string\n  author: string\n  created_at: Date\n  archived: boolean\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput\n```\n\n## Features\n\n### Offline-First\n\nPowerSync collections are offline-first by default. All data is stored locally in a SQLite database, allowing your app to work without an internet connection. Changes are automatically synced when connectivity is restored.\n\n### Real-Time Sync\n\nWhen connected to a PowerSync backend, changes are automatically synchronized in real-time across all connected clients. The sync process handles:\n\n- Bi-directional sync with the server\n- Conflict resolution\n- Queue management for offline changes\n- Automatic retries on connection loss\n\n### Query-Driven Sync\nCollections support an optional `on-demand` sync mode that loads only the rows your active live queries need, rather than syncing the full table into memory upfront. Useful for large datasets where most rows won't be accessed at a given time.\n\n### Working with Rich JavaScript Types\n\nPowerSync collections support rich JavaScript types like `Date`, `Boolean`, and custom objects while maintaining SQLite compatibility. The collection handles serialization and deserialization automatically:\n\n```typescript\nimport { z } from \"zod\"\nimport { Schema, Table, column } from \"@powersync/web\"\nimport { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\n// Define PowerSync SQLite schema\nconst APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  tasks: new Table({\n    title: column.text,\n    due_date: column.text, // Stored as ISO string in SQLite\n    completed: column.integer, // Stored as 0/1 in SQLite\n    metadata: column.text, // Stored as JSON string in SQLite\n  }),\n})\n\n// Define rich types schema\nconst taskSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  title: z.string().nullable(),\n  due_date: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? new Date(val) : null)), // Convert to Date\n  completed: z\n    .number()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val != null ? val > 0 : null)), // Convert to boolean\n  metadata: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? JSON.parse(val) : null)), // Parse JSON\n})\n\n// Create collection with rich types\nconst tasksCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.tasks,\n    schema: taskSchema,\n  })\n)\n\n// Work with rich types in your code\nawait tasksCollection.insert({\n  id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n  title: \"Review PR\",\n  due_date: \"2025-10-30T10:00:00Z\", // String input is automatically converted to Date\n  completed: 0, // Number input is automatically converted to boolean\n  metadata: JSON.stringify({ priority: \"high\" }),\n})\n\n// Query returns rich types\nconst task = tasksCollection.get(\"task-1\")\nconsole.log(task.due_date instanceof Date) // true\nconsole.log(typeof task.completed) // \"boolean\"\nconsole.log(task.metadata.priority) // \"high\"\n```\n\n### Type Safety with Rich Types\n\nThe collection maintains type safety throughout:\n\n```typescript\ntype TaskInput = {\n  id: string\n  title: string | null\n  due_date: string | null // Accept ISO string for mutations\n  completed: number | null // Accept 0/1 for mutations\n  metadata: string | null // Accept JSON string for mutations\n}\n\ntype TaskOutput = {\n  id: string\n  title: string | null\n  due_date: Date | null // Get Date object in queries\n  completed: boolean | null // Get boolean in queries\n  metadata: {\n    priority: string\n    [key: string]: any\n  } | null\n}\n\n// TypeScript enforces correct types:\ntasksCollection.insert({\n  due_date: new Date(), // Error: Type 'Date' is not assignable to type 'string'\n})\n\nconst task = tasksCollection.get(\"task-1\")\ntask.due_date.getTime() // OK - TypeScript knows this is a Date\n```\n\n### Optimistic Updates\n\nUpdates to the collection are applied optimistically to the local state first, then synchronized with PowerSync and the backend. If an error occurs during sync, the changes are automatically rolled back.\n\n### Metadata Tracking\n\nMetadata tracking allows attaching custom metadata to collection operations (insert, update, delete). This metadata is persisted alongside the operation and available in PowerSync `CrudEntry` records during upload processing. This is useful for passing additional context about mutations to the backend, such as audit information, operation sources, or custom processing hints.\n\n#### Enabling Metadata Tracking\n\nMetadata tracking must be enabled on the PowerSync table:\n\n```typescript\nconst APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  documents: new Table(\n    {\n      name: column.text,\n      author: column.text,\n    },\n    {\n      // Enable metadata tracking on this table\n      trackMetadata: true,\n    }\n  ),\n})\n```\n\n#### Using Metadata in Operations\n\nOnce enabled, metadata can be passed to any collection operation:\n\n```typescript\nconst documents = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n  })\n)\n\n// Insert with metadata\nawait documents.insert(\n  {\n    id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n    name: \"Report Q4\",\n    author: \"Jane Smith\",\n  },\n  {\n    metadata: {\n      source: \"web-app\",\n      userId: \"user-123\",\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\n    },\n  }\n).isPersisted.promise\n\n// Update with metadata\nawait documents.update(\n  docId,\n  { metadata: { reason: \"typo-fix\", editor: \"user-456\" } },\n  (doc) => {\n    doc.name = \"Report Q4 (Updated)\"\n  }\n).isPersisted.promise\n\n// Delete with metadata\nawait documents.delete(docId, {\n  metadata: { deletedBy: \"user-789\", reason: \"duplicate\" },\n}).isPersisted.promise\n```\n\n#### Accessing Metadata During Upload\n\nThe metadata is available in PowerSync `CrudEntry` records when processing uploads in the connector:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { CommonPowerSyncDatabase } from \"@powersync/web\"\n\nclass Connector implements PowerSyncBackendConnector {\n  // ...\n\n  async uploadData(database: CommonPowerSyncDatabase) {\n    const batch = await database.getCrudBatch()\n    if (!batch) return\n\n    for (const entry of batch.crud) {\n      console.log(\"Operation:\", entry.op) // PUT, PATCH, DELETE\n      console.log(\"Table:\", entry.table)\n      console.log(\"Data:\", entry.opData)\n      console.log(\"Metadata:\", entry.metadata) // Custom metadata (stringified)\n\n      // Parse metadata if needed\n      if (entry.metadata) {\n        const meta = JSON.parse(entry.metadata)\n        console.log(\"Source:\", meta.source)\n        console.log(\"User ID:\", meta.userId)\n      }\n\n      // Process the operation with the backend...\n    }\n\n    await batch.complete()\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Note**: If metadata is provided to an operation but the table doesn't have `trackMetadata: true`, a warning will be logged and the metadata will be ignored.\n\n## Configuration Options\n\nThe `powerSyncCollectionOptions` function accepts the following options:\n\n```ts\ninterface PowerSyncCollectionConfig<TTable extends Table, TSchema> {\n  // Required options\n  database: PowerSyncDatabase\n  table: Table\n\n  // Schema validation and type transformation\n  schema?: StandardSchemaV1\n  deserializationSchema?: StandardSchemaV1 // Required for custom input types\n  onDeserializationError?: (error: StandardSchemaV1.FailureResult) => void // Required for custom input types\n\n  // Optional Custom serialization\n  serializer?: {\n    [Key in keyof TOutput]?: (value: TOutput[Key]) => SQLiteCompatibleType\n  }\n\n  // Performance tuning\n  syncBatchSize?: number // Control batch size for initial sync, defaults to 1000\n}\n```\n\n## Advanced Transactions\n\nWhen you need more control over transaction handling, such as batching multiple operations or handling complex transaction scenarios, you can use PowerSync's transaction system directly with TanStack DB transactions.\n\n```ts\nimport { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { PowerSyncTransactor } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\n// Create a transaction that won't auto-commit\nconst batchTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Use PowerSyncTransactor to apply the transaction to PowerSync\n    await new PowerSyncTransactor({ database: db }).applyTransaction(\n      transaction\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// Perform multiple operations in the transaction\nbatchTx.mutate(() => {\n  // Add multiple documents in a single transaction\n  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    documentsCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      name: `Document ${i}`,\n      content: `Content ${i}`,\n      created_at: new Date().toISOString(),\n      updated_at: new Date().toISOString(),\n    })\n  }\n})\n\n// Commit the transaction\nawait batchTx.commit()\n\n// Wait for the changes to be persisted\nawait batchTx.isPersisted.promise\n```\n\nThis approach allows you to:\n\n- Batch multiple operations into a single transaction\n- Control when the transaction is committed\n- Ensure all operations are atomic\n- Wait for persistence confirmation\n- Handle complex transaction scenarios\n\n## On-demand Sync Mode (Query-Driven Sync)\n\nBy default the PowerSync collection uses the `eager` sync mode, but you can opt to use `on-demand` sync mode instead. In on-demand mode, the collection is query-driven - it only loads data from SQLite that satisfies the predicates of your active live queries, rather than syncing everything and filtering afterwards.\n\nTo understand how data flows under the hood, it helps to know that there are two independent dimensions at play:\n\n- **Sync mode** (`eager` vs `on-demand`) - controls how data moves from SQLite into the TanStack DB collection\n- **Sync Streams** (optional) - controls how data moves from the PowerSync Service into SQLite, using the `onLoad` or `onLoadSubset` hooks\n\nThese dimensions compose independently, giving you six combinations covered below:\n\n| Example | Sync mode | Sync Streams | Parameters |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Eager | No | - |\n| 2 | On-demand | No | - |\n| 3 | Eager | Yes | Hardcoded |\n| 4 | On-demand | Yes | Hardcoded |\n| 5 | On-demand | Yes | Dynamic - `extractSimpleComparisons` |\n| 6 | On-demand | Yes | Dynamic - `parseWhereExpression` |\n\nExamples 1 and 2 assume SQLite is already fully populated by PowerSync's sync mechanism. Examples 3–6 add Sync Streams, which let you control which data from the PowerSync Service reaches SQLite in the first place - useful when you only want a subset of data on-device.\n\nIn examples 3 and 4, Sync Stream subscription parameters are hardcoded. Examples 5 and 6 show how to derive those parameters dynamically from the live query's predicates, so the subscription automatically adjusts as the query changes.\n\n\n### 1. Eager Mode\n\nIn `eager` mode, all data from the SQLite database is synced into the TanStack DB collection. The live query then filters this full dataset to produce the final result. This means the collection holds all rows regardless of whether they match any active query.\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Eager mode syncs all SQLite data to TanStack DB Collection*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'eager'\n  }),\n)\n\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n### 2. On-Demand Mode\n\nIn `on-demand` mode, only data from the SQLite database that satisfies the predicates of active TanStack DB live queries is synced into the TanStack DB collection. This reduces the amount of data held in memory, since the collection only contains rows that are relevant to the queries currently registered against it.\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand'\n  }),\n)\n\n// live query that tells the collection what the data domain is\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n## Incorporating Sync Streams With OnLoad/OnLoadSubset Hooks\nSync Streams can be used through the data loading hooks for both `eager` and `on-demand` sync modes that allow a user to call Sync Streams when a collection is defined (eager mode) or when a collection's data boundary changes based on the live queries predicates (on-demand).\n\nThese examples assume the following Sync Stream definition exists:\n```\nconfig:\n  edition: 3\n\nstreams:\n  lists:\n    query: SELECT * FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id()\n    auto_subscribe: true\n  todos:\n    query: SELECT * FROM todos WHERE list_id = subscription.parameter('list') AND list_id IN (SELECT id FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id())\n```\n\n### 3. Eager Mode with Sync Streams\n\nUse the `onLoad` hook to subscribe to a Sync Stream when the collection first loads, so SQLite is populated before the collection starts serving queries. In `eager` mode, all rows in SQLite are synced into the TanStack DB collection; live query filtering then runs against that full dataset. \nThe hook can optionally return a cleanup function where you can unsubscribe from the Sync Stream.\n\nThis example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, only 2 of which get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Because it's eager mode, both get synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query only returns the single todo that matches the live query predicate.\n\n**PS Service**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*Eager mode syncs all SQLite data to TanStack DB Collection*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for completed = 1*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'eager',\n    onLoad: async () => {\n      console.log('onLoad')\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: 'list_1' })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log('onUnload')\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n) \n\n// A live query that filters by the `completed` state.\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.completed, 1))\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n### 4. On-Demand Mode with Sync Streams\n\nUse the `onLoadSubset` hook to subscribe to a Sync Stream whenever the collection's data boundary changes (i.e. when the set of active live queries changes). In `on-demand` mode, only rows that satisfy the active live query predicates are synced from SQLite into the TanStack DB collection. \nThe hook can optionally return a cleanup function where you can unsubscribe from the Sync Stream when that subset is no longer needed.\n\nThis example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, only 2 of which get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Because it's on-demand mode, only 1 todo matches gets synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query only returns the single todo that matches the live query predicate.\n\n**PS Service**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: completed = 1*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for completed = 1*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      console.log('onLoadSubset')\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: 'list_1' })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log('onUnloadSubset')\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// A live query that filters by the `completed` state.\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.completed, 1))\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n### 5. On-Demand Mode with Simple Predicates\n\nIn the previous example, the Sync Stream subscription parameters were hardcoded. In practice, you'll often want to derive those parameters dynamically from the live query's where clause. That way, changing the query automatically adjusts which data is synced from the PowerSync Service. `extractSimpleComparisons` is a convenience helper that parses the expression tree from `onLoadSubset` into a flat list of `{ field, operator, value }` objects. \n\nGiven a live query like:\n\n```\n.where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, selectedListId))\n```\n\n`onLoadSubset` receives options.where as an expression tree `for eq(list_id, '<uuid>')`.\nThe `list_id` value is parsed from the expression tree and passed to `syncStream()`.\n\nNote: This example slightly differs from the previous two as it aims to illustrate `extractSimpleComparisons` usage.\n\nThis example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, the Sync Stream subscription criteria (`list_id = \"list_1\"`) is derived from the live query registered against the collection. Only 2 todos get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Two todos get synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query returns both todos as they both match `eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id')`.\n\n**PS Service**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Derive the list_id criteria from live query, subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list = 'list_1'*\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      // Extract simple comparisons from the where expression\n      const comparisons = extractSimpleComparisons(options.where)\n      // comparisons = [{ field: ['todo', 'list_id'], operator: 'eq', value: '<uuid>' }]\n\n      // Find the list_id filter\n      const listIdFilter = comparisons.find(\n        (c) => c.field.includes('list_id') && c.operator === 'eq',\n      )\n\n      if (!listIdFilter) {\n        console.warn('No list_id filter found, skipping Sync Stream')\n        return\n      }\n\n      console.log(`Subscribing to todos for list: ${listIdFilter.value}`)\n\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: listIdFilter.value })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log(`Unsubscribing from todos for list: ${listIdFilter.value}`)\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// Simple filter -> triggers `onLoadSubset` with `eq(list_id, '...')`\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'), // or some listId variable\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n### 6. On-Demand Mode with Complex Predicates\n\nWhile `extractSimpleComparisons` works well for simple, single-condition filters, live queries often combine multiple conditions using `and`, `or`, or other nested expressions. `parseWhereExpression` lets you provide custom handler functions for each operator (`eq`, `and`, etc.). This gives you full control over how a compound expression tree is unpacked and transformed into the parameters object passed to `syncStream()`. It's useful when your Sync Stream accepts multiple parameters and you need to extract all of them from a single composite where clause - for example, filtering by both `list_id` and `completed` status simultaneously.\n\nAssume a small adjustment to the Sync Stream definition of todos (adding the `completed` subscription parameter):\n```\ntodos:\n    query: SELECT * FROM todos WHERE list_id = subscription.parameter('list') AND completed = subscription.parameter(\"completed\") AND list_id IN (SELECT id FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id())\n```\n\nThe `list` parameter name is kept as-is (consistent with most examples), but must be mapped to `list_id` to work with the following example. Alternatively, it can be named `list_id` in the Sync Stream definition to skip the programmatic mapping step.\n\nThis example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, the Sync Stream subscription criteria (`list_id = \"list_1\" and completed = 1`) is derived from the live query registered against the collection. Only 1 todo gets synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. One todo gets synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query returns 1 todo that matches `eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id') and eq(todo.completed, 1)`.\n\n**PS Service**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Derive the list_id and completed criteria from live query, subscribe to Sync Stream where list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1*\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      // Parse the where into a flat params record using custom handlers\n      const streamParams = parseWhereExpression(options.where, {\n        handlers: {\n          eq: (field: Array<string>, value: unknown) => {\n            const mappedField = mapFields(field[field.length - 1]!)\n\n            return {\n              [mappedField]: value,\n            }\n          },\n          and: (...filters: Array<Record<string, unknown>>) =>\n            Object.assign({}, ...filters),\n        },\n        onUnknownOperator: (op, _args) => {\n          console.warn(`Ignoring unsupported operator in stream params: ${op}`)\n          return {}\n        },\n      })\n      // For a query like: where(({ todo }) => and(eq(todo.list_id, 'abc'), eq(todo.completed, 0)))\n      // streamParams = { list: 'abc', completed: 0 }\n\n      if (!streamParams || Object.keys(streamParams).length === 0) {\n        console.warn('No stream params extracted, skipping Sync Stream')\n        return\n      }\n\n      console.log(\n        `Subscribing to todos with params: ${JSON.stringify(streamParams)}`,\n      )\n\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', streamParams)\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => subscription.unsubscribe()\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// Compound filter -> triggers `onLoadSubset` with `and(eq(list_id, '...'), eq(completed, 1))`\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) =>\n        and(eq(todo.list_id, 'list_1'), eq(todo.completed, 1)),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: PowerSync Collection\n---\n\nPowerSync collections provide seamless integration between TanStack DB and [PowerSync](https://powersync.com), enabling automatic synchronization between your in-memory TanStack DB collections and PowerSync's SQLite database. This gives you offline-ready persistence, real-time sync capabilities, and powerful conflict resolution.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `@tanstack/powersync-db-collection` package allows you to create collections that:\n\n- Automatically mirror the state of an underlying PowerSync SQLite database\n- Reactively update when PowerSync records change\n- Support query-driven sync, loading only the data relevant to active live queries\n- Support optimistic mutations with rollback on error\n- Provide persistence handlers to keep PowerSync in sync with TanStack DB transactions\n- Use PowerSync's efficient SQLite-based storage engine\n- Work with PowerSync's real-time sync features for offline-first scenarios\n- Leverage PowerSync's built-in conflict resolution and data consistency guarantees\n- Enable real-time synchronization with PostgreSQL, MongoDB and MySQL backends\n\n## 1. Installation\n\nInstall the PowerSync collection package along with your preferred framework integration.\nPowerSync currently works with Web, React Native and Node.js. The examples below use the Web SDK.\nSee the PowerSync quickstart [docs](https://docs.powersync.com/installation/quickstart-guide) for more details.\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/powersync-db-collection @powersync/web @journeyapps/wa-sqlite\n```\n\n### 2. Create a PowerSync Database and Schema\n\n```ts\nimport { Schema, Table, column } from \"@powersync/web\"\n\n// Define your schema\nconst APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  documents: new Table({\n    name: column.text,\n    author: column.text,\n    created_at: column.text,\n    archived: column.integer,\n  }),\n})\n\n// Initialize PowerSync database\nconst db = new PowerSyncDatabase({\n  database: {\n    dbFilename: \"app.sqlite\",\n  },\n  schema: APP_SCHEMA,\n})\n```\n\n### 3. (optional) Configure Sync with a Backend\n\n```ts\nimport type {\n  CommonPowerSyncDatabase,\n  PowerSyncBackendConnector,\n  PowerSyncCredentials,\n} from \"@powersync/web\"\n\n// TODO implement your logic here\nclass Connector implements PowerSyncBackendConnector {\n  fetchCredentials: () => Promise<PowerSyncCredentials | null>\n\n  /** Upload local changes to the app backend.\n   *\n   * Use {@link CommonPowerSyncDatabase.getCrudBatch} to get a batch of changes to upload.\n   *\n   * Any thrown errors will result in a retry after the configured wait period (default: 5 seconds).\n   */\n  uploadData: (database: CommonPowerSyncDatabase) => Promise<void>\n}\n\n// Configure the client to connect to a PowerSync service and your backend\ndb.connect(new Connector())\n```\n\n### 4. Create a TanStack DB Collection\n\nThere are two main ways to create a collection: using type inference or using schema validation. Type inference will infer collection types from the underlying PowerSync SQLite tables. Schema validation can be used for additional input/output validations and type transforms.\n\n#### Option 1: Using Table Type Inference\n\nThe collection types are automatically inferred from the PowerSync schema table definition. The table is used to construct a default standard schema validator which is used internally to validate collection operations.\n\nCollection mutations accept SQLite types and queries report data with SQLite types.\n\n```ts\nimport { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: string | null // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number | null // SQLite integer\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput\n```\n\nThe standard PowerSync SQLite types map to these TypeScript types:\n\n| PowerSync Column Type | TypeScript Type  | Description                                                          |\n| --------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `column.text`         | `string \\| null` | Text values, commonly used for strings, JSON, dates (as ISO strings) |\n| `column.integer`      | `number \\| null` | Integer values, also used for booleans (0/1)                         |\n| `column.real`         | `number \\| null` | Floating point numbers                                               |\n\nNote: All PowerSync column types are nullable by default.\n\n#### Option 2: SQLite Types with Schema Validation\n\nAdditional validations for collection mutations can be performed with a custom schema. The Schema below asserts that\nthe `name`, `author` and `created_at` fields are required as input. `name` also has an additional string length check.\n\nNote: The input and output types specified in this example still satisfy the underlying SQLite types. An additional `deserializationSchema` is required if the typing differs. See the examples below for more details.\n\nThe application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the `schema`. Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An `onDeserializationError` handler must be provided to react to this case.\n\n```ts\nimport { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\nimport { z } from \"zod\"\n\n// Schema validates SQLite types but adds constraints\nconst schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string().min(3, { message: \"Should be at least 3 characters\" }),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.string(), // SQLite TEXT for dates\n  archived: z.number(),\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string\n  author: string\n  created_at: string // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number // SQLite integer\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput\n```\n\n#### Option 3: Transform SQLite Input Types to Rich Output Types\n\nYou can transform SQLite types to richer types (like Date objects) while keeping SQLite-compatible input types:\n\nNote: The Transformed types are provided by TanStack DB to the PowerSync SQLite persister. These types need to be serialized in\norder to be persisted to SQLite. Most types are converted by default. For custom types, override the serialization by providing a\n`serializer` param.\n\nThe example below uses `nullable` columns, this is not a requirement.\n\nThe application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the `schema`. Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An `onDeserializationError` handler must be provided to react to this case.\n\n```ts\nconst schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string().nullable(),\n  created_at: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? new Date(val) : null)), // Transform SQLite TEXT to Date\n  archived: z\n    .number()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val != null ? val > 0 : null)), // Transform SQLite INTEGER to boolean\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n    // Optional: custom column serialization\n    serializer: {\n      // Dates are serialized by default, this is just an example\n      created_at: (value) => (value ? value.toISOString() : null),\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: string | null // SQLite TEXT\n  archived: number | null\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string | null\n  author: string | null\n  created_at: Date | null // JS Date instance\n  archived: boolean | null // JS boolean\n}\n```\n\n#### Option 4: Custom Input/Output Types with Deserialization\n\nThe input and output types can be completely decoupled from the internal SQLite types. This can be used to accept rich values for input mutations.\nWe require an additional `deserializationSchema` in order to validate and transform incoming synced (SQLite) updates. This schema should convert the incoming SQLite update to the output type.\n\nThe application logic (including the backend) should enforce that all incoming synced data passes validation with the `deserializationSchema`. Failing to validate data will result in inconsistency of the collection data. This is a fatal error! An `onDeserializationError` handler must be provided to react to this case.\n\n```ts\n// Our input/output types use Date and boolean\nconst schema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string(),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.date(), // Accept Date objects as input\n  archived: z.boolean(), // Accept Booleans as input\n})\n\n// Schema to transform from SQLite types to our output types\nconst deserializationSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string(),\n  author: z.string(),\n  created_at: z\n    .string()\n    .transform((val) => (new Date(val))), // SQLite TEXT to Date\n  archived: z\n    .number()\n    .transform((val) => (val > 0), // SQLite INTEGER to Boolean\n})\n\nconst documentsCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n    schema,\n    deserializationSchema,\n    onDeserializationError: (error) => {\n      // Present fatal error\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n/** Note: The types for input and output are defined as this */\n// Used for mutations like `insert` or `update`\ntype DocumentCollectionInput = {\n  id: string\n  name: string\n  author: string\n  created_at: Date\n  archived: boolean\n}\n// The type of query/data results\ntype DocumentCollectionOutput = DocumentCollectionInput\n```\n\n## Features\n\n### Offline-First\n\nPowerSync collections are offline-first by default. All data is stored locally in a SQLite database, allowing your app to work without an internet connection. Changes are automatically synced when connectivity is restored.\n\n### Real-Time Sync\n\nWhen connected to a PowerSync backend, changes are automatically synchronized in real-time across all connected clients. The sync process handles:\n\n- Bi-directional sync with the server\n- Conflict resolution\n- Queue management for offline changes\n- Automatic retries on connection loss\n\n### Query-Driven Sync\nCollections support an optional `on-demand` sync mode that loads only the rows your active live queries need, rather than syncing the full table into memory upfront. Useful for large datasets where most rows won't be accessed at a given time.\n\n### Working with Rich JavaScript Types\n\nPowerSync collections support rich JavaScript types like `Date`, `Boolean`, and custom objects while maintaining SQLite compatibility. The collection handles serialization and deserialization automatically:\n\n```typescript\nimport { z } from \"zod\"\nimport { Schema, Table, column } from \"@powersync/web\"\nimport { createCollection } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { powerSyncCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\n// Define PowerSync SQLite schema\nconst APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  tasks: new Table({\n    title: column.text,\n    due_date: column.text, // Stored as ISO string in SQLite\n    completed: column.integer, // Stored as 0/1 in SQLite\n    metadata: column.text, // Stored as JSON string in SQLite\n  }),\n})\n\n// Define rich types schema\nconst taskSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  title: z.string().nullable(),\n  due_date: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? new Date(val) : null)), // Convert to Date\n  completed: z\n    .number()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val != null ? val > 0 : null)), // Convert to boolean\n  metadata: z\n    .string()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform((val) => (val ? JSON.parse(val) : null)), // Parse JSON\n})\n\n// Create collection with rich types\nconst tasksCollection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.tasks,\n    schema: taskSchema,\n  })\n)\n\n// Work with rich types in your code\nawait tasksCollection.insert({\n  id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n  title: \"Review PR\",\n  due_date: \"2025-10-30T10:00:00Z\", // String input is automatically converted to Date\n  completed: 0, // Number input is automatically converted to boolean\n  metadata: JSON.stringify({ priority: \"high\" }),\n})\n\n// Query returns rich types\nconst task = tasksCollection.get(\"task-1\")\nconsole.log(task.due_date instanceof Date) // true\nconsole.log(typeof task.completed) // \"boolean\"\nconsole.log(task.metadata.priority) // \"high\"\n```\n\n### Type Safety with Rich Types\n\nThe collection maintains type safety throughout:\n\n```typescript\ntype TaskInput = {\n  id: string\n  title: string | null\n  due_date: string | null // Accept ISO string for mutations\n  completed: number | null // Accept 0/1 for mutations\n  metadata: string | null // Accept JSON string for mutations\n}\n\ntype TaskOutput = {\n  id: string\n  title: string | null\n  due_date: Date | null // Get Date object in queries\n  completed: boolean | null // Get boolean in queries\n  metadata: {\n    priority: string\n    [key: string]: any\n  } | null\n}\n\n// TypeScript enforces correct types:\ntasksCollection.insert({\n  due_date: new Date(), // Error: Type 'Date' is not assignable to type 'string'\n})\n\nconst task = tasksCollection.get(\"task-1\")\ntask.due_date.getTime() // OK - TypeScript knows this is a Date\n```\n\n### Optimistic Updates\n\nUpdates to the collection are applied optimistically to the local state first, then synchronized with PowerSync and the backend. If an error occurs during sync, the changes are automatically rolled back.\n\n### Metadata Tracking\n\nMetadata tracking allows attaching custom metadata to collection operations (insert, update, delete). This metadata is persisted alongside the operation and available in PowerSync `CrudEntry` records during upload processing. This is useful for passing additional context about mutations to the backend, such as audit information, operation sources, or custom processing hints.\n\n#### Enabling Metadata Tracking\n\nMetadata tracking must be enabled on the PowerSync table:\n\n```typescript\nconst APP_SCHEMA = new Schema({\n  documents: new Table(\n    {\n      name: column.text,\n      author: column.text,\n    },\n    {\n      // Enable metadata tracking on this table\n      trackMetadata: true,\n    }\n  ),\n})\n```\n\n#### Using Metadata in Operations\n\nOnce enabled, metadata can be passed to any collection operation:\n\n```typescript\nconst documents = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: APP_SCHEMA.props.documents,\n  })\n)\n\n// Insert with metadata\nawait documents.insert(\n  {\n    id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n    name: \"Report Q4\",\n    author: \"Jane Smith\",\n  },\n  {\n    metadata: {\n      source: \"web-app\",\n      userId: \"user-123\",\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\n    },\n  }\n).isPersisted.promise\n\n// Update with metadata\nawait documents.update(\n  docId,\n  { metadata: { reason: \"typo-fix\", editor: \"user-456\" } },\n  (doc) => {\n    doc.name = \"Report Q4 (Updated)\"\n  }\n).isPersisted.promise\n\n// Delete with metadata\nawait documents.delete(docId, {\n  metadata: { deletedBy: \"user-789\", reason: \"duplicate\" },\n}).isPersisted.promise\n```\n\n#### Accessing Metadata During Upload\n\nThe metadata is available in PowerSync `CrudEntry` records when processing uploads in the connector:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { CommonPowerSyncDatabase } from \"@powersync/web\"\n\nclass Connector implements PowerSyncBackendConnector {\n  // ...\n\n  async uploadData(database: CommonPowerSyncDatabase) {\n    const batch = await database.getCrudBatch()\n    if (!batch) return\n\n    for (const entry of batch.crud) {\n      console.log(\"Operation:\", entry.op) // PUT, PATCH, DELETE\n      console.log(\"Table:\", entry.table)\n      console.log(\"Data:\", entry.opData)\n      console.log(\"Metadata:\", entry.metadata) // Custom metadata (stringified)\n\n      // Parse metadata if needed\n      if (entry.metadata) {\n        const meta = JSON.parse(entry.metadata)\n        console.log(\"Source:\", meta.source)\n        console.log(\"User ID:\", meta.userId)\n      }\n\n      // Process the operation with the backend...\n    }\n\n    await batch.complete()\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Note**: If metadata is provided to an operation but the table doesn't have `trackMetadata: true`, a warning will be logged and the metadata will be ignored.\n\n## Configuration Options\n\nThe `powerSyncCollectionOptions` function accepts the following options:\n\n```ts\ninterface PowerSyncCollectionConfig<TTable extends Table, TSchema> {\n  // Required options\n  database: PowerSyncDatabase\n  table: Table\n\n  // Schema validation and type transformation\n  schema?: StandardSchemaV1\n  deserializationSchema?: StandardSchemaV1 // Required for custom input types\n  onDeserializationError?: (error: StandardSchemaV1.FailureResult) => void // Required for custom input types\n\n  // Optional Custom serialization\n  serializer?: {\n    [Key in keyof TOutput]?: (value: TOutput[Key]) => SQLiteCompatibleType\n  }\n\n  // Performance tuning\n  syncBatchSize?: number // Control batch size for initial sync, defaults to 1000\n}\n```\n\n## Advanced Transactions\n\nWhen you need more control over transaction handling, such as batching multiple operations or handling complex transaction scenarios, you can use PowerSync's transaction system directly with TanStack DB transactions.\n\n```ts\nimport { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { PowerSyncTransactor } from \"@tanstack/powersync-db-collection\"\n\n// Create a transaction that won't auto-commit\nconst batchTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Use PowerSyncTransactor to apply the transaction to PowerSync\n    await new PowerSyncTransactor({ database: db }).applyTransaction(\n      transaction\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// Perform multiple operations in the transaction\nbatchTx.mutate(() => {\n  // Add multiple documents in a single transaction\n  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    documentsCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      name: `Document ${i}`,\n      content: `Content ${i}`,\n      created_at: new Date().toISOString(),\n      updated_at: new Date().toISOString(),\n    })\n  }\n})\n\n// Commit the transaction\nawait batchTx.commit()\n\n// Wait for the changes to be persisted\nawait batchTx.isPersisted.promise\n```\n\nThis approach allows you to:\n\n- Batch multiple operations into a single transaction\n- Control when the transaction is committed\n- Ensure all operations are atomic\n- Wait for persistence confirmation\n- Handle complex transaction scenarios\n\n## On-demand Sync Mode (Query-Driven Sync)\n\nBy default the PowerSync collection uses the `eager` sync mode, but you can opt to use `on-demand` sync mode instead. In on-demand mode, the collection is query-driven - it only loads data from SQLite that satisfies the predicates of your active live queries, rather than syncing everything and filtering afterwards.\n\nTo understand how data flows under the hood, it helps to know that there are two independent dimensions at play:\n\n- **Sync mode** (`eager` vs `on-demand`) - controls how data moves from SQLite into the TanStack DB collection\n- **Sync Streams** (optional) - controls how data moves from the PowerSync Service into SQLite, using the `onLoad` or `onLoadSubset` hooks\n\nThese dimensions compose independently, giving you six combinations covered below:\n\n| Example | Sync mode | Sync Streams | Parameters |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Eager | No | - |\n| 2 | On-demand | No | - |\n| 3 | Eager | Yes | Hardcoded |\n| 4 | On-demand | Yes | Hardcoded |\n| 5 | On-demand | Yes | Dynamic - `extractSimpleComparisons` |\n| 6 | On-demand | Yes | Dynamic - `parseWhereExpression` |\n\nExamples 1 and 2 assume SQLite is already fully populated by PowerSync's sync mechanism. Examples 3–6 add Sync Streams, which let you control which data from the PowerSync Service reaches SQLite in the first place - useful when you only want a subset of data on-device.\n\nIn examples 3 and 4, Sync Stream subscription parameters are hardcoded. Examples 5 and 6 show how to derive those parameters dynamically from the live query's predicates, so the subscription automatically adjusts as the query changes.\n\n\n### 1. Eager Mode\n\nIn `eager` mode, all data from the SQLite database is synced into the TanStack DB collection. The live query then filters this full dataset to produce the final result. This means the collection holds all rows regardless of whether they match any active query.\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Eager mode syncs all SQLite data to TanStack DB Collection*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'eager'\n  }),\n)\n\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n### 2. On-Demand Mode\n\nIn `on-demand` mode, only data from the SQLite database that satisfies the predicates of active TanStack DB live queries is synced into the TanStack DB collection. This reduces the amount of data held in memory, since the collection only contains rows that are relevant to the queries currently registered against it.\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand'\n  }),\n)\n\n// live query that tells the collection what the data domain is\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n## Incorporating Sync Streams With OnLoad/OnLoadSubset Hooks\nSync Streams can be used through the data loading hooks for both `eager` and `on-demand` sync modes that allow a user to call Sync Streams when a collection is defined (eager mode) or when a collection's data boundary changes based on the live queries predicates (on-demand).\n\nThese examples assume the following Sync Stream definition exists:\n```\nconfig:\n  edition: 3\n\nstreams:\n  lists:\n    query: SELECT * FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id()\n    auto_subscribe: true\n  todos:\n    query: SELECT * FROM todos WHERE list_id = subscription.parameter('list') AND list_id IN (SELECT id FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id())\n```\n\n### 3. Eager Mode with Sync Streams\n\nUse the `onLoad` hook to subscribe to a Sync Stream when the collection first loads, so SQLite is populated before the collection starts serving queries. In `eager` mode, all rows in SQLite are synced into the TanStack DB collection; live query filtering then runs against that full dataset. \nThe hook can optionally return a cleanup function where you can unsubscribe from the Sync Stream.\n\nThis example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, only 2 of which get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Because it's eager mode, both get synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query only returns the single todo that matches the live query predicate.\n\n**PS Service**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*Eager mode syncs all SQLite data to TanStack DB Collection*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for completed = 1*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'eager',\n    onLoad: async () => {\n      console.log('onLoad')\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: 'list_1' })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log('onUnload')\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n) \n\n// A live query that filters by the `completed` state.\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.completed, 1))\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n### 4. On-Demand Mode with Sync Streams\n\nUse the `onLoadSubset` hook to subscribe to a Sync Stream whenever the collection's data boundary changes (i.e. when the set of active live queries changes). In `on-demand` mode, only rows that satisfy the active live query predicates are synced from SQLite into the TanStack DB collection. \nThe hook can optionally return a cleanup function where you can unsubscribe from the Sync Stream when that subset is no longer needed.\n\nThis example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, only 2 of which get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Because it's on-demand mode, only 1 todo matches gets synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query only returns the single todo that matches the live query predicate.\n\n**PS Service**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: completed = 1*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for completed = 1*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      console.log('onLoadSubset')\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: 'list_1' })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log('onUnloadSubset')\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// A live query that filters by the `completed` state.\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.completed, 1))\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n### 5. On-Demand Mode with Simple Predicates\n\nIn the previous example, the Sync Stream subscription parameters were hardcoded. In practice, you'll often want to derive those parameters dynamically from the live query's where clause. That way, changing the query automatically adjusts which data is synced from the PowerSync Service. `extractSimpleComparisons` is a convenience helper that parses the expression tree from `onLoadSubset` into a flat list of `{ field, operator, value }` objects. \n\nGiven a live query like:\n\n```\n.where(({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, selectedListId))\n```\n\n`onLoadSubset` receives options.where as an expression tree `for eq(list_id, '<uuid>')`.\nThe `list_id` value is parsed from the expression tree and passed to `syncStream()`.\n\nNote: This example slightly differs from the previous two as it aims to illustrate `extractSimpleComparisons` usage.\n\nThis example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, the Sync Stream subscription criteria (`list_id = \"list_1\"`) is derived from the live query registered against the collection. Only 2 todos get synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. Two todos get synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query returns both todos as they both match `eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id')`.\n\n**PS Service**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Derive the list_id criteria from live query, subscribe to Sync Stream, only sync where list = 'list_1'*\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for list_id = 'list_1'*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      // Extract simple comparisons from the where expression\n      const comparisons = extractSimpleComparisons(options.where)\n      // comparisons = [{ field: ['todo', 'list_id'], operator: 'eq', value: '<uuid>' }]\n\n      // Find the list_id filter\n      const listIdFilter = comparisons.find(\n        (c) => c.field.includes('list_id') && c.operator === 'eq',\n      )\n\n      if (!listIdFilter) {\n        console.warn('No list_id filter found, skipping Sync Stream')\n        return\n      }\n\n      console.log(`Subscribing to todos for list: ${listIdFilter.value}`)\n\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', { list: listIdFilter.value })\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => {\n        console.log(`Unsubscribing from todos for list: ${listIdFilter.value}`)\n        subscription.unsubscribe()\n      }\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// Simple filter -> triggers `onLoadSubset` with `eq(list_id, '...')`\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(\n        ({ todo }) => eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id'), // or some listId variable\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n\n### 6. On-Demand Mode with Complex Predicates\n\nWhile `extractSimpleComparisons` works well for simple, single-condition filters, live queries often combine multiple conditions using `and`, `or`, or other nested expressions. `parseWhereExpression` lets you provide custom handler functions for each operator (`eq`, `and`, etc.). This gives you full control over how a compound expression tree is unpacked and transformed into the parameters object passed to `syncStream()`. It's useful when your Sync Stream accepts multiple parameters and you need to extract all of them from a single composite where clause - for example, filtering by both `list_id` and `completed` status simultaneously.\n\nAssume a small adjustment to the Sync Stream definition of todos (adding the `completed` subscription parameter):\n```\ntodos:\n    query: SELECT * FROM todos WHERE list_id = subscription.parameter('list') AND completed = subscription.parameter(\"completed\") AND list_id IN (SELECT id FROM lists WHERE owner_id = auth.user_id())\n```\n\nThe `list` parameter name is kept as-is (consistent with most examples), but must be mapped to `list_id` to work with the following example. Alternatively, it can be named `list_id` in the Sync Stream definition to skip the programmatic mapping step.\n\nThis example starts with 4 todos in the PowerSync Service, the Sync Stream subscription criteria (`list_id = \"list_1\" and completed = 1`) is derived from the live query registered against the collection. Only 1 todo gets synced via the Sync Stream to the SQLite database. One todo gets synced from the SQLite database to the collection. Finally the TanStack DB query returns 1 todo that matches `eq(todo.list_id, 'list_id') and eq(todo.completed, 1)`.\n\n**PS Service**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 1  | list_1  |     0     |\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n| 3  | list_2  |     0     |\n| 4  | list_2  |     1     |\n\n*Derive the list_id and completed criteria from live query, subscribe to Sync Stream where list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1*\n\n**SQLite**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*On-demand mode only syncs data from SQLite to the TanStack DB collection if they satisfy any of the live queries: list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1*\n\n**Collection**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n*TanStack DB Query filters for list = 'list_1' AND completed = 1*\n\n**Result**\n\n| id | list_id | completed |\n|:--:|:-------:|:---------:|\n| 2  | list_1  |     1     |\n\n```typescript\nconst collection = createCollection(\n  powerSyncCollectionOptions({\n    database: db,\n    table: AppSchema.props.todos,\n    syncMode: 'on-demand',\n    onLoadSubset: async (options) => {\n      // Parse the where into a flat params record using custom handlers\n      const streamParams = parseWhereExpression(options.where, {\n        handlers: {\n          eq: (field: Array<string>, value: unknown) => {\n            const mappedField = mapFields(field[field.length - 1]!)\n\n            return {\n              [mappedField]: value,\n            }\n          },\n          and: (...filters: Array<Record<string, unknown>>) =>\n            Object.assign({}, ...filters),\n        },\n        onUnknownOperator: (op, _args) => {\n          console.warn(`Ignoring unsupported operator in stream params: ${op}`)\n          return {}\n        },\n      })\n      // For a query like: where(({ todo }) => and(eq(todo.list_id, 'abc'), eq(todo.completed, 0)))\n      // streamParams = { list: 'abc', completed: 0 }\n\n      if (!streamParams || Object.keys(streamParams).length === 0) {\n        console.warn('No stream params extracted, skipping Sync Stream')\n        return\n      }\n\n      console.log(\n        `Subscribing to todos with params: ${JSON.stringify(streamParams)}`,\n      )\n\n      const subscription = await db\n        .syncStream('todos', streamParams)\n        .subscribe()\n\n      await subscription.waitForFirstSync()\n\n      return () => subscription.unsubscribe()\n    },\n  }),\n)\n\n// Compound filter -> triggers `onLoadSubset` with `and(eq(list_id, '...'), eq(completed, 1))`\nconst liveQuery = createLiveQueryCollection({\n  query: (q) =>\n    q\n      .from({ todo: collection })\n      .where(({ todo }) =>\n        and(eq(todo.list_id, 'list_1'), eq(todo.completed, 1)),\n      )\n      .select(({ todo }) => ({\n        id: todo.id,\n        completed: todo.completed,\n      })),\n})\n```\n"}
